  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "lem:airy-tube-permutation-orbit-bayes-lower",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "Assume \\(\\alpha_n(3T_k^\\dagger+4\\varepsilon)\\le1\\).  Under the face-robust permutation-invariant prior\n\\(\\nu_{n,k,\\varepsilon}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A}\\), every outcome-independent assignment law \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) and every possibly randomized labeled-data estimator have Bayes risk at least\n\\[\n\\sum_{r\\in\\mathcal R_{n,3}}\\pi(r)\n\\mathcal B_{n,k,\\varepsilon}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A}(r)\n\\ge B_{n,k,\\varepsilon}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\pi\\) is the allocation-count law induced by \\(\\mathcal D_n\\).  Conditional on a fixed allocation count, the posterior mean of the exact finite-population target attains the displayed fixed-count Bayes risk.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "k",
      "\\varepsilon",
      "\\alpha_n",
      "T_k^\\dagger",
      "\\nu_{n,k,\\varepsilon}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A}",
      "\\mathcal D_n",
      "\\widehat\\tau",
      "r",
      "\\mathcal R_{n,3}",
      "\\pi",
      "\\mathcal B_{n,k,\\varepsilon}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A}(r)",
      "B_{n,k,\\varepsilon}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A}"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:outcome-independent-design",
      "prop:unequal-three-arm-exact-primitives",
      "def:three-arm-orbit-likelihood",
      "def:face-robust-directional-ledger",
      "def:explicit-three-arm-airy-tube",
      "lem:face-robust-tangent-score-identities",
      "def:exact-airy-tube-bayes-risk"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The face-robust tangent-score lemma proves from the positivity premise that every conditional vector\n\\(p_{n,x,h,\\zeta}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A}\\) is a probability distribution and proves that its count target is the exact finite-population contrast.  Conditional on the three coordinates, drawing a multinomial count and then choosing a labeling uniformly in its orbit is equivalent to drawing the labeled response types independently.  Indeed, for a labeled type sequence with count vector \\(m\\), the product of its multinomial mass and the reciprocal orbit size is\n\\[\n\\left\\{\\frac{n!}{\\prod_tm_t!}\\prod_t\n(p_{n,x,h,\\zeta,t}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A})^{m_t}\\right\\}\n\\left\\{\\frac{\\prod_tm_t!}{n!}\\right\\}\n=\\prod_{i=1}^np_{n,x,h,\\zeta,t_i}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFix an assignment vector \\(a_0\\) with count vector \\(r\\).  Given a response-count vector \\(m\\), the prior is uniform over all labeled type sequences in that orbit.  Counting the type-by-arm tables compatible with the observed arm-success vector gives exactly\n\\(P_m(X^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\mid r)\\) from the orbit-likelihood definition.  Two labeled outcome vectors with the same arm-success counts differ by a permutation within assigned-arm blocks.  Both the prior and the exact target are invariant under that permutation.  Therefore the posterior distribution of the target depends on the labeled observations only through \\((r,X^{\\mathrm{obs}})\\).\n\nFor any action \\(d\\), conditional square completion gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\{(d-\\tau)^2\\mid r,X^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\}\n=\\operatorname{Var}(\\tau\\mid r,X^{\\mathrm{obs}})\n+\\{d-\\mathbb E(\\tau\\mid r,X^{\\mathrm{obs}})\\}^2.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe identity remains valid after averaging estimator randomization.  Thus the posterior mean minimizes conditional squared error, and summing its posterior variance with the exact likelihood gives\n\\(\\mathcal B_{n,k,\\varepsilon}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A}(r)\\) by the exact Airy-tube Bayes-risk definition.\n\nNow let \\(A\\sim\\mathcal D_n\\).  Outcome independence ensures that conditioning on an assignment vector does not change the schedule prior.  Any two assignment vectors with the same count are related by a common unit permutation, so the minimum conditional Bayes risk is the same and equals the fixed-count value above.  Applying (2) conditionally on every assignment vector and grouping by the induced count law \\(\\pi\\) gives the first inequality in the statement.  Its right side is a convex average of the fixed-count risks and is therefore at least their minimum\n\\(B_{n,k,\\varepsilon}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm A}\\).  This proves the claim for arbitrary dependence across assignments and arbitrary randomized labeled-data estimators.",
    "justification": "Permutation invariance and posterior square completion transfer the three-coordinate prior losslessly to every dependent labeled assignment design.",
    "gap": "This exact finite reduction does not provide an asymptotic expansion.",
    "consumer": "The fixed-input evaluator and all-design root use the Bayes lower certificate.",
